 could form  maybe it's forming right over here let  me do that in a different color  maybe it's forming right over here and  so when you have this platform right  over here where it's going to do is it's  going to reduce it's going to reduce  blood flow to all of these parts let me  draw that a little bit neater it will  reduce blood flow to all of this kind of  downstream part of the artery and  because it's reducing blood flow all of  the muscle around here all of the  muscles that gets its oxygen that's fed  oxygen from kind of the downstream part  of the artery they  get enough oxygen and they won't be able  to pump properly and so the main symptom  of coronary artery disease is called  angina pectoris  you might have so angina and Jona  pectoris and it's usually just called  angina for short  but angina pectoris literally means a  strangling like feeling in the chest and  you get that strangling like feeling in  the chest because your heart is not  getting enough oxygen to pump properly  but at this point you still don't have a  heart attack a heart attack happens  where one of these plaques are unstable  enough so they might have a little  membrane covering the top of it but  they're unstable enough so that they  eventually because of the because of the  blood flow then the pressure from the  blood they eventually break off so they  eventually break off and go into the  blood so then you have all of the  material enters in the blood and then  the blood actually clots around it and  so the blood actually clots around it so  here the blood is clotting around all of  these materials so you had if plaque now  it was unstable and so at some point it  just randomly breaks off all of all of  this blood clots around this and then  it's going downstream it's going  downstream where the arteries get  thinner and thinner and thinner so you  can imagine if this thing right here was  unstable it could get dislodged over  there let me do this in a color we can  see it gets dislodged over there you  still can't see that color it gets  dislodged over there and if it goes  downstream the arteries get narrow  narrow and at some point it could get it  could it could it'll get stuck in a  thinner part of the artery so a good  downstream until it gets stuck and at  that point is completely blocking that  artery and this completely blocking of  the artery from a kind of a blood clot  that came off of one of these unstable  plaques this is called thrombosis so  this right here is thrombosis thrombosis  so it's the blocking of an artery due to  a plaque but once you block that artery  then not none of the none of the none of  the muscles that that artery was feeding  can now get oxygen  everything downstream from that will no  longer be able to get oxygen and so what  you'll see is that this muscle over here  that needs that oxygen it will start to  die it will start to it will start to  infarct and I know this is a very  strange word infarct is literally it  dead is literally dead tissue so what  you have over here some of this muscle  tissue on the heart that's not getting  proper blood anymore it experiences  infarction or the or the proper term for  it is myocardial infarction myocardial  myocardial infarction and this is a very  fancy word but really it's just saying  myocardial is actually the muscle tissue  of the heart infarction mean it's dying  so part of the heart the muscle tissue  of the heart is dying over here and so  when you have mato when you have  myocardial infarction all of a sudden  because this happens this isn't  something that happens slowly remember  this the plaque might form slowly over  over years over decades but eventually  becomes unstable and then one day just  breaks apart and this whole time you  might have been experiencing something  called stable angina stable angina means  hey you got this thing over here  you're usually okay but if you start  jogging maybe you start getting a little  bit of chest pain th